<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manifesto of the Algerian People</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manifesto of the Algerian People was a declaration drawn up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferhat-abbas">Ferhat Abbas</a> and presented to the French authorities in February 1943, after the Allied landings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. It condemned colonial rule and called for an Algerian constitution guaranteeing equality, an end to land seizures and recognition of Arabic. It marked the shift of moderate nationalists from assimilation towards self-government.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under German occupation and Anglo-American forces landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-torch">Operation Torch</a> in 1942, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferhat-abbas">Ferhat Abbas</a> set out to unite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>'s political forces, among them elected members of the Algerian People's Party and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, around a common programme. On 17 January 1943 members of the party and of the ulama met with several Muslim representatives at the house of the lawyer and politician Ahmed Boumendjel in Algiers. Abbas was asked to write the text, which he did in Setif, in a room above his pharmacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Published on 10 February 1943 and signed by 28 elected Muslim officials, the manifesto called for a new status for the Algerian nation and asserted the right of the Algerian people to govern themselves. Besides a constitution guaranteeing liberty and equality regardless of race and religion and official status for Arabic beside French, it demanded freedom of religion with the separation of church and state. An addendum called for an Algerian Assembly at the end of the war and proposed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> become an autonomous state federated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, reached through negotiation with the French authorities. In the words of the historian Benjamin Stora, it established the political majority of the Algerian people, drawing on the followers of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messali-hadj">Messali Hadj</a> and Abbas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The addendum went to the governor-general Peyrouton in May 1943. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, on his arrival, called for an end to the project, and on 23 June 1943 General Catroux, the new governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, rejected the manifesto. In March 1944 its supporters founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of the Manifesto and Liberty, known by its French initials AML, to defend it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manifesto of the Algerian People” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
